```python
def _update_next(self, current_time):
    if self._next_timeout <= current_time:
        callback_time_sec = self.callback_time / 1000.0
        time_missed = current_time - self._next_timeout
        if time_missed > callback_time_sec:
            self._next_timeout += (math.floor(time_missed / callback_time_sec) + 1) * callback_time_sec
        else:
            self._next_timeout += callback_time_sec
```
This updated function first checks if the `_next_timeout` has been reached. If it has, it calculates the `time_missed` by subtracting `_next_timeout` from `current_time`. It then checks if `time_missed` is greater than `callback_time_sec`. If it is, it adds the difference to `_next_timeout` in increments of `callback_time_sec`. If not, it simply adds `callback_time_sec` to `_next_timeout`. This ensures that the function will always run at the correct interval, even if the system clock changes.
